== README R9's measurement hazard, tested rather than inherited ==

R9 states: "on JDK 21 and later a single `String.format` with a numeric conversion
permanently deoptimises every char narrowing loop in the process, which is protobuf-java's
own encoder."

Every harness in this slice formats after its last measurement because of that sentence.
This is the check that the sentence describes something real here. It does -- **it is
larger than stated, it is on the JDK the rule exempts and not the one it names, its sign
depends on the arm, and one arm is immune** -- and none of those four is what the rule says.

`-Dak.deopt=` selects what the process does BEFORE its first measurement:

  0  nothing
  1  one `String.format("%d%n", 1)`, which is what R9 names
  2  20,000 `String.charAt` calls on a three-character WIDE String (above U+00FF)
  3  20,000 `String.charAt` calls on a three-character LATIN1 String

Modes 2 and 3 carry no Formatter, no numeric conversion and no narrowing loop. They exist
because mode 1 moved protobuf-java by a factor of two and a mystery that large has to be
narrowed to a mechanism before it is reported.

## 1. protobuf-java's own encode, P1.2, `base ns/op`

| jdk | content set | deopt=0 | deopt=1 | deopt=2 | deopt=3 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 17 | ASCII | 510,636 | 490,332 | 577,450 | 488,212 |
| **17** | **above U+00FF** | **1,314,490** | **600,612** | 1,244,272 | **604,567** |
| 21 | ASCII | 487,096 | 477,732 | 490,706 | 490,403 |
| 21 | above U+00FF | 1,266,444 | 1,301,174 | 1,239,299 | 1,302,017 |

Repeated four times on the row that moves, JDK 17 above U+00FF, in microseconds:

    deopt=0   1297 1338 1302 1328
    deopt=1    586  587  606  602
    deopt=2   1248 1265 1251 1260
    deopt=3    604  600  604  633

**Stable, and the trigger is not what R9 names.** Reading a LATIN1 String's characters
does it; reading a WIDE String's characters does not; and `String.format` does it because
it reads Latin-1 strings internally, not because it formats a number. There is no
narrowing loop in modes 2 or 3 at all.

*Recorded because it was seen*: one earlier single run of mode 2 read 610 us where these
four read 1248 to 1265. One reading against four, and it is written down rather than
dropped.

## 2. It is not one arm moving. It is two arms moving OPPOSITE WAYS, and a third immune.

Absolutes on JDK 17, above U+00FF, P1.2, ns/op:

| arm | deopt=0 | deopt=3 | change |
|---|---|---|---|
| `pbj` (toByteArray, fresh message) | 1,297,600 | 601,283 | **2.16x faster** |
| `pbj-reused-out` | 738,334 | 359,567 | 2.05x faster |
| `pbj-loop` | 794,131 | 402,860 | 1.97x faster |
| `pbj-reuse` | 700,704 | 310,863 | 2.25x faster |
| `pbj-det` | 696,811 | 318,680 | 2.19x faster |
| `pbj-parsed` | 1,348,206 | 606,093 | 2.22x faster |
| **`R`** (the generated Java codec) | **598,193** | **757,617** | **1.27x SLOWER** |
| `R-take` | 631,931 | 787,080 | 1.25x slower |
| **`ffi`** (the C ABI) | **674,752** | **642,772** | **1.05x faster, i.e. unmoved** |
| `ffi-nobatch` | 617,657 | 642,772 | unmoved |
| `ffi-zeroed` | 664,371 | 658,405 | unmoved |
| `ffi-take` | 718,870 | 728,154 | unmoved |

**Every arm that transcodes UTF-16 in Java moves, and the two that do move go opposite
ways. The arm that transcodes in the core does not move at all.**

That is what makes the mechanism legible. protobuf-java's `Utf8.encode` and this slice's
`ak.Utf8.encode` are both `charAt` loops over a `String`, so both are compiled against the
same compact-string dispatch profile, and that profile can only be specialised one way. Pre-
reading a Latin-1 string changes which way. The C ABI arm has no such loop -- ABI v1
section 4's whole argument is that "the core implements all of them once for every
language, and every managed host stops maintaining a UTF-8 encoder" -- so there is nothing
in it for the profile to specialise.

## 3. What this says about R9, and what the slice did about it

The rule's operational advice is right and this slice follows it: no harness here formats
anything until after its last measurement. What the rule gets wrong is everything else.

* **The JDK.** R9 says "JDK 21 and later". It reproduces on **17** and not on 21; on 21 all
  four modes agree within 5 percent.
* **The trigger.** R9 says `String.format` with a numeric conversion. It is any read of a
  Latin-1 String's characters, and `String.format` is one instance of that.
* **The mechanism.** R9 says a char NARROWING loop is deoptimised. Modes 2 and 3 contain no
  narrowing loop and differ only in the probe string's coder, and only one of them fires.
* **The sign.** R9 says the incumbent gets slower. Here the incumbent gets 2.2 times
  FASTER and the generated Java codec gets 1.27 times slower.
* **The content set.** Nothing happens on ASCII, on either JDK. R9 does not say so, and
  every published managed figure taken on ASCII alone is therefore not exposed to this --
  which is also why the effect has stayed invisible.

**The consequence for the branch is larger than the correction.** A managed slice's
string-path number depends on what its process read before it, by a factor of two, in a
direction that differs between two arms of the same slice. A ratio between a Java-side
encoder and protobuf-java on non-ASCII content is not a stable quantity on JDK 17 unless
the harness says what warmed the process -- and no published report says.

**And the C ABI's immunity is a result, not a footnote.** Moving the transcoder into the
core is what makes the arm insensitive to the host JIT's profile history. That is an
argument for ABI v1 section 4 that no benchmark was looking for.

## 4. What this does not establish

* One machine, one protobuf-java (3.25.5), one payload (P1.2), one JDK 17 build.
* The JIT mechanism is inferred from which triggers fire and which arms move, not from a
  compilation log. `-XX:+PrintCompilation` and `-XX:+TraceDeoptimization` would settle it
  and were not run.
* Nothing here tests the hazard as R9 describes it on the JDK it describes it for, with the
  protobuf-java version it was seen on. A failure to reproduce is not a refutation, and the
  rule is kept.
